WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.533
Swap a and b: a moving drops D(a), b moving drops D(b).

2
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:17.444
But both count the a–b edge as “fixed”, and it still crosses after the swap: 2w too many.

3
00:00:17.444 --> 00:00:24.467
E↔D: 9 − 10 = −1; counting the cut directly, 12 → 13, it agrees.

4
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:30.333
An eight-node graph from an alternating seed: cut 7.

5
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.578
The best first step is only −1.

6
00:00:33.578 --> 00:00:38.378
But step two is +7: keep two steps, cut 1.

7
00:00:38.378 --> 00:00:43.622
A greedy that accepts only gains would stop at 7 right away.

8
00:00:44.022 --> 00:00:50.156
Why must a prefix be consecutive? Each gain depends on the steps before it.

9
00:00:50.156 --> 00:00:56.956
Pass 2's D↔B is +2 in sequence, but −12 alone from the start.

10
00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:01.156
So take only the first k steps; k = 0 is valid too.

11
00:01:01.556 --> 00:01:05.689
Change the seed: three seeds reach cut 3.

12
00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:10.933
But AB | CDE stays at 5: both trials lose.

13
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:16.622
Reaching DE | ABC needs two pairs at once: a local optimum.

14
00:01:17.022 --> 00:01:20.489
Give A area 3 and the rest 1.

15
00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:26.844
After A↔D the count is still 2 to 3, but the area goes from 4 : 3 to 2 : 5.

16
00:01:26.844 --> 00:01:31.200
KL keeps only the count; area needs its own check.

17
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.178
A pass has n/2 steps.

18
00:01:34.178 --> 00:01:37.867
Each step looks at up to (n/2)² pairs.

19
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:43.333
A naive pass is about n³; sorting by D prunes a lot.

20
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:48.800
Next module's FM moves one node at a time, for a near-linear pass.

21
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.267
Six things to keep.

22
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.178
Next: move one node at a time.
